# React & Next.js coding style

## Core principle: KISS (Keep It Stupid Simple)

- Organize, name, and design for simplicity. Page routes are thin parent components: parent-level state only, parent-level hooks only, children are reusable functional components that only need the props they use.
- No unnecessary prop drilling. Each component is self-contained and works the same regardless of parent as long as it gets the right props (or none).

## File and function size

- **Target**: Every function visible on a normal-sized screen without scrolling hundreds of lines.
- **Rough limit**: Files/methods over ~300 lines are a bad smell (exceptions: CSS, generated config). TSX and route components should stay small.
- Split large logic into hooks, components, or constants; keep route parents tiny and delegate to sections.

## Naming

- **Components**: PascalCase (`About.tsx`, `TeamTable`).
- **Constants** (static lists, config): UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (`MY_LIST`, `CARD_LIST`).
- **Hooks**: camelCase with `use` prefix (`useAuth`, `useApi`).
- **Types/interfaces**: PascalCase. No `any` or `unknown` — use proper types, generics, and extend PrimeReact/React types where relevant.

## TypeScript

- **Use TypeScript fully**: generics, extending interfaces, strict types. Never use `any` or `unknown`; use proper types or type guards.
- Extend library types when wrapping (e.g. PrimeReact `ButtonProps`) and keep types in `@/types` with index re-exports. Enforce with linter.

## Structure (this codebase)

- **Routes**: Next.js App Router — each route is `app/<route>/page.tsx` (the parent for that page). Keep it thin: state/hooks at parent, then section components.
- **Components**: `@/components` — common, layout, ui, pages. Hierarchical `index.ts` so imports use `@/components` (not deep paths).
- **Constants**: `@/constants` — static copy, nav, theme, etc. One concern per file; re-export from `constants/index.ts`.
- **Types**: `@/types` — re-export from `types/index.ts`; keep types next to features when feature-specific.
- **Hooks, services, stores**: `@/hooks`, `@/services`, `@/stores` with index re-exports. Use React state + context + Zustand as appropriate; avoid unnecessary rerenders and expensive work on render.

## Components and data

- Prefer list/data patterns like `<CardListView data={CARD_LIST} />` with `data.map(item => <Card key={item.id} ... />)` so content is data-driven and ready for API/JSON.
- Constants for lists live in their own files under `constants/` (e.g. `CARD_LIST` in a dedicated or domain file). Keep TSX clean; no raw HTML blobs for copy — use imported constants.

## Performance

- Avoid unnecessary rerenders; keep state flow minimal and updates predictable.
- Do not call APIs or heavy work on every render; use caching, effects with correct deps, or data layer (stores/services) as appropriate.

## Refactoring

- Prefer “rip it out”: remove what’s not needed, verify behavior, then reorganize and refactor in new locations. Keep PRs/commits focused on one feature or fix.

## Version and docs

- Update CHANGELOG for code changes; keep README accurate and dev-facing. Bump version in package.json per changelog (minor/patch as appropriate).
